rolling-energy-title = A ball’s kinetic and potential energy
rolling-energy-question = We weigh a steel ball and let it go on a groove 5, 10 and 20 cm above the table. Having rolled down, it rolls along the table past two chalk marks 50 cm apart, and a phone films it in slow motion — 240 frames a second. From the frames we find the speed, work out the kinetic energy m·v²/2 and set it against the potential energy m·g·h the ball lost. What share of the ball’s potential energy becomes kinetic, m·v²/2?
rolling-energy-choices = All of it: energy is conserved — m·v²/2 = m·g·h
rolling-energy-choices-2 = Noticeably less than all — and the same share from any height
rolling-energy-choices-3 = Only a small share: friction eats almost all
rolling-energy-asides = In 1722 the Dutchman Willem ’s Gravesande dropped weights from different heights into soft clay. A weight twice as fast dug a hole not twice but about four times as deep: the energy of motion grows with the square of the speed. The French scholar Émilie du Châtelet explained the experiment and was the first to publish that the energy is proportional to m·v². The very words «kinetic energy» came only in the mid-19th century — from William Thomson (Lord Kelvin).
rolling-energy-answer-why =
    Energy does not vanish or come from nowhere — it changes from one form to another. Rolling down, the ball loses the potential energy m·g·h. But where does it go? Not only into going forward: the ball also spins, and spinning has kinetic energy too. Rolling without slipping, a solid ball gives its spin 2/5 of the energy of its forward motion, so the forward motion keeps m·g·h / (1 + 2/5) = 5/7 ≈ 71 %. This share depends neither on the height nor on the ball’s mass or size — only on its being solid.

    Rolling friction is small here — so neither «all» nor «almost none». But if the ball did not roll but slid without friction (like a puck on ice), all of m·g·h would become m·v²/2.
rolling-energy-answer-say = The kinetic energy is only ≈ { $mean } % of the potential — the same from every height!
rolling-energy-answer-say-2 = Let us compare the energies.
